SMART HOME SYSTEM – FULL IMPLEMENTATION AND CONNECTION GUIDE

You are building a complete smart home research system using three ESP32 boards that communicate through MQTT and are controlled by a dashboard application.

The goal is to make sure:

• All three ESP32 boards are connected correctly
• All sensors and actuators work
• All ESP32 devices communicate using MQTT
• The dashboard receives real-time data
• The dashboard can control all devices
• Emergency events trigger actions across systems

You must carefully implement the following steps and verify that everything works.

---

STEP 1 — SYSTEM OVERVIEW

The system contains three ESP32 controllers:

ESP32 #1 — Home Energy System
ESP32 #2 — Garage Automation
ESP32 #3 — Smart Garden System

All ESP32 devices connect to the SAME WiFi network and the SAME MQTT broker.

The dashboard also connects to the same MQTT broker to display data and send commands.

Communication Flow

Sensors → ESP32 → MQTT Broker → Dashboard

Dashboard Commands → MQTT Broker → ESP32 → Device Action

Also:

ESP32 → MQTT → Other ESP32

Example:

If smoke is detected in the garden system, the garden ESP32 sends a message through MQTT and the home and garage ESP32 open their doors automatically.

---

STEP 2 — HARDWARE CONNECTIONS

You must connect all sensors and devices to the ESP32 boards.

HOME ESP32 (Energy System)

Devices connected:

3 LEDs → digital pins
RGB LED → PWM pins
DHT11/DHT22 sensor → data pin
RTC module DS3231 → I2C pins
Servo motor → PWM pin
Magnetic reed switch → digital input
PIR motion sensor → digital input
Battery level sensor → analog pin (optional)

Make sure all grounds are shared and power is correct.

GARAGE ESP32

Devices connected:

Servo motor → garage door
Ultrasonic sensor HC-SR04 → car detection
Bluetooth scanning → built into ESP32
Optional buzzer for warnings

GARDEN ESP32

Devices connected:

Soil moisture sensor → analog pin
Water pump relay → digital output
MQ-2 smoke sensor → analog input
Ultrasonic sensor → water tank level
Buzzer → alarm output
Warning LED → digital output

After connecting hardware, verify each sensor individually.

